#: core.ptm_builder.load_image:1 of
msgid "Decode one capture as an (height, width, 3) uint8 array."
msgstr ""

#: core.ptm_builder.generate_native:1 of
msgid "Fit the PTM in-process and write it to `destination`."
msgstr ""

#: core.ptm_builder.generate_native:3 of
msgid ""
"Unlike `generate` this has no opinion about paths: nothing shells out, so"
" spaces and non-ASCII anywhere are simply not a problem. It also has no "
"size ceiling beyond available memory, which is the reason it exists — "
"`PTMfitter.exe` is 32-bit and fails above about 24 megapixels."
msgstr ""

#: core.ptm_builder.generate_native:12 of
msgid "Where the .ptm goes."
msgstr ""

#: core.ptm_builder.generate_native:14 of
msgid ""
"Optional `progress(done, total)`, called per image. The fit reads every "
"capture, so this is not instant."
msgstr ""

#: core.ptm_builder.generate_native:16 of
msgid "Seam for tests; called with a path, returns an image array."
msgstr ""

#: core.ptm_builder.generate_native:18 of
msgid ""
"The .lp written beside the images. Not needed by the fit — kept because "
"it is the record of which light each shot was taken under."
msgstr ""

#: core.settings:1 of
msgid "Preference keys, defaults and coercion."
msgstr ""

#: core.settings:3 of
msgid ""
"QSettings stores everything as text in an .ini file, so a value written "
"as an int comes back as a string. The defaults and the coercion live here"
" so the two windows that read preferences cannot drift apart on either."
msgstr ""

#: core.settings.FITTER_NATIVE:1 of
msgid ""
"How the .ptm is produced. \"native\" fits in-process and has no size "
"limit beyond memory; \"external\" shells out to PTMfitter.exe, which is "
"32-bit and fails above about 24 megapixels. External is kept selectable "
"while the native path proves itself against real captures."
msgstr ""

#: core.settings.value_to_bool:1 of
msgid "Coerce a QSettings value to bool."
msgstr ""

#: core.settings.value_to_bool:3 of
msgid ""
"An .ini round-trips True as the string \"true\", which is truthy either "
"way, but so is \"false\"."
msgstr ""

